Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Period gothic live-action. An OLD MAN, frail and white-haired in a long nightshirt, sleeps beneath a heavy four-poster bed. His pale blue eye bears a milky film.

At the door stands NARRATOR, a lean, pale man in a dark waistcoat and black coat, holding a closed tin lantern.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

The NARRATOR's hand does not tremble as he turns the latch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Sunlight falls across a neat chamber. The OLD MAN sits awake beside a small table of untouched treasures.

The NARRATOR enters warmly, carrying a breakfast tray. His dark coat and waistcoat are immaculate.

NARRATOR
Good morning, sir. How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN smiles faintly. He turns his filmed blue eye toward the NARRATOR.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The NARRATOR freezes under the old man's gaze, then resumes his pleasant smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

The OLD MAN reaches for the tray. The NARRATOR watches the eye, cold and intent.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The room is black as pitch. Shutters are fastened tight.

The NARRATOR eases the door open a fraction and slips his closed lantern through the gap.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

Across seven nights, the same careful ritual repeats: the latch turns; the lantern enters; the NARRATOR's pale face advances inch by inch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The NARRATOR puts his head through the opening. He opens the lantern only enough for one thin ray.

The OLD MAN sleeps. His eye remains shut.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

The lantern closes. The NARRATOR silently withdraws.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

The OLD MAN sits at breakfast. The NARRATOR enters with the same bright manner.

NARRATOR
Good morning, sir. How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN answers with an uncertain nod.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. The NARRATOR opens the door more slowly than a watch's minute hand moves.

A tiny, involuntary chuckle catches in his throat.

The OLD MAN bolts upright in bed.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The NARRATOR becomes utterly still in the doorway.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

The OLD MAN remains upright, listening into the blackness.

A low groan slips from him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

The OLD MAN clutches his blanket. His eyes search the darkness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been trying to fancy them causeless, but could not. “It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.”

The NARRATOR opens a minute crevice in the lantern. A thread of dim light shoots across the room.

It falls full upon the OLD MAN'S open eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

The pale blue eye gleams beneath its hideous veil.

A low, dull, quick beat begins beneath the silence.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

The NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless. The heartbeat grows quicker.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The OLD MAN gasps. The beat grows louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!

The NARRATOR's breath shortens. His eyes gleam.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room.

The OLD MAN shrieks once.

The NARRATOR drags him to the floor and heaves the heavy bed over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

Under the bed, the heartbeat continues, muffled but relentless.

The NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, listening with a dreadful smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

The beating slows. Stops.

The NARRATOR lifts the bed. The OLD MAN lies still. He lays his hand over the dead man's heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

A covered tub stands beside the bed. The NARRATOR works in silence, sleeves rolled above his wrists.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence.

He removes three floor planks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The NARRATOR deposits the concealed remains between the scantlings. He replaces the planks with meticulous care.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

He wipes his hands. The room is clean. The bed is restored.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

EXT. OLD MAN'S STREET DOOR - PRE-DAWN

The street is empty and dark. A bell tolls four.

Three composed policemen wait at the door: OFFICER REYNOLDS, broad-shouldered in a dark uniform; OFFICER VALE, narrow-faced and watchful; and OFFICER BLAKE, older, carrying a notebook.

The NARRATOR opens the door with a light heart.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard during the night. We are deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

The NARRATOR opens the door wide.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen, you are welcome. Search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER VALE, and OFFICER BLAKE inspect the room. They examine the undisturbed treasures and the fastened shutters.

The NARRATOR follows them easily, smiling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I took my visitors all over the house. I led them, at length, to his chamber. I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed.

The officers finish their search. Their suspicion eases.

The NARRATOR brings in chairs. He places his own chair directly over the concealed planks and sits.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

OFFICER BLAKE sits opposite him. OFFICER REYNOLDS and OFFICER VALE settle nearby. They chat pleasantly.

NARRATOR
You find all in order, gentlemen?

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Entirely in order.

A faint ringing begins in the NARRATOR's ears. His smile holds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted.

The ringing sharpens into a low, dull, quick sound.

The NARRATOR glances down at the floorboards.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The heartbeat rises beneath his chair.

The officers continue their calm conversation. They do not react.

NARRATOR gasps, then speaks faster.

NARRATOR
The weather has been most unseasonable of late.

The heartbeat grows louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness.

The NARRATOR rises. He paces over the boards with heavy strides.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased.

The heartbeat pounds, louder, louder.

The NARRATOR swings his chair and grates it across the floorboards.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer!

OFFICER VALE watches him, concerned but silent.

The heartbeat overwhelms the room.

NARRATOR
Villains!

The officers rise.

NARRATOR
Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The NARRATOR points wildly at the floor.

The three officers stare at the planks as the heartbeat thunders.

FADE OUT.
